# Minor keys and the raised seventh: the relative minor found on a major scale's 6th note, the
# harmonic minor's raised 7th, the semitones it changes, minor key signatures, a tune's key, and one
# of the child's own, by a fiddle in the research hut.
lesson music-minor-keys v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=3 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Minor keys and the raised seventh"
  goal "Find a major key's relative minor and build the harmonic minor with its raised 7th."

  look {
    scene 36x14 {
      notes scale clef=treble notes=[A4, B4, C5, D5, E5, F5, Gs5, A5] values=[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1] meter=0 letters=true at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      In the research hut, in the long Arctic summer, Ben plays fiddle tunes in minor keys. Every
      major key has a relative minor that uses the same notes and the same key signature, starting
      on the major scale's 6th note: C major and A minor share their notes. A natural minor scale
      ends with a whole tone from its 7th note up to home. To make the ending pull home, the
      harmonic minor raises the 7th by a semitone, so A minor's G becomes G sharp, written in the
      music as it comes.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.minor-relative k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show music.minor-seventh k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level easy {
      worked music.minor-sig k=0 level=medium
    }
    show music.minor-steps q=0 hard-q=1
    show music.minor-play
    show music.minor-sig k=1 hard-k=2
    show music.minor-relative k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show music.minor-which k=0 hard-k=1
    show music.minor-seventh k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    level medium hard {
      show music.minor-steps q=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.minor-why r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.minor-compose v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A relative minor starts on the major scale's 6th note and shares its key signature; the harmonic minor raises the 7th a semitone to lead home."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that every major key has a relative minor that shares its notes and key signature,
    starting on the major scale's 6th note, and that the harmonic minor raises its 7th note a
    semitone so it leads strongly to the home note. The step up from grade 6's key signatures, which
    were major only with one question on the relative minor, is building the minor scale itself and
    the raised 7th, and reading minor key signatures through their majors. The wrong ideas to watch
    for are writing the raised 7th into the key signature, and naming the relative minor without its
    sharp (F minor for F sharp minor). In the working, look for the child numbering a scale's notes
    from 1. The core finds relative minors, raises the 7th, counts the semitones it changes, plays A
    harmonic minor, gives minor key signatures and names a tune's key; the two-star asks why the 7th
    is raised, and the three-star is a minor tune of the child's own.

    The theory is from Wikipedia, "Minor scale": the natural minor shares its key signature with the
    relative major a third above; the harmonic minor raises the 7th to give a leading tone, which
    leaves an awkward augmented second between the 6th and 7th. If the relative minor is stuck, play
    C major then A minor on the white keys and hear the same notes start from a new home. Off the
    page, sing a song you know in a minor key and stop on the note before the end. This lesson meets
    Russia's federal work programme in music for primary school, 「Мажорные и минорные тональности
    (до 2–3 знаков при ключе)」 (major and minor keys with up to two or three sharps or flats), and
    Japan's 2017 course of study for music, grades 5 and 6, A(1)ウ(ア), singing from 「ハ長調及びイ短調の楽譜」
    (scores in C major and A minor), both as quoted in the audit of grades 5 and 6.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the relative minors are of C and G major, the 7ths raised are in A and E minor, the key signature of A minor is worked once in the teacher's pen, the steps counted are the 7th to home in both minors, the tune's key is A minor, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the tune is four bars in A minor."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the relative minors are of D and A major, the 7ths raised are in B and D minor, the steps counted include the awkward step from F to G sharp, the key signature is of B minor, the tunes' keys include one in C major to tell apart, and the tune is eight bars in E minor."
  }
}
